    <innerclass refid="classPharmacies" prot="public">Pharmacies</innerclass>
    <briefdescription>
<para><ref refid="classService" kindref="compound">Service</ref> entity bound to the <computeroutput>pharmacies</computeroutput> table (a pharmacy service provider). </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>This header declares the <ref refid="classPharmacies" kindref="compound">Pharmacies</ref> class, a very small C++ type that stands for one pharmacy in the <computeroutput>pharmacies</computeroutput> database table. It holds almost no logic of its own. It only names the table and lets its parent class <ref refid="classService" kindref="compound">Service</ref> do the real work of building SQL statements. You create one by passing in a data wrapper made from a client request, and the parent stores that wrapper for later use.</para>
<para>WHY THIS FILE MATTERS IN THE SERVER: <ref refid="classPharmacies" kindref="compound">Pharmacies</ref> is one of the &quot;service provider&quot; types in the server, next to laboratories and radiology centres. When a client creates, reads, updates or deletes a pharmacy, or manages its staff, the route layer picks this class as <ref refid="classServiceController" kindref="compound">ServiceController&lt;Pharmacies&gt;</ref> / <ref refid="classStaffController" kindref="compound">StaffController&lt;Pharmacies&gt;</ref>. Those controllers call the inherited methods from <ref refid="classService" kindref="compound">Service</ref> and <ref refid="classEntity" kindref="compound">Entity</ref>, which produce the actual SQL. The class also publishes the table name so <ref refid="gatekeeper_8cpp_1ae17e951f1dc80a8e4920571ebe9f3c21" kindref="member">PermissionManager</ref> can check that the requesting staff member is allowed to change this service. Without this file there would be no C++ type tying the <computeroutput>pharmacies</computeroutput> table to the generic CRUD machinery, so pharmacy endpoints could not work.</para>
<para>CONCEPTS TO LEARN (for beginners):<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>static constexpr: a value fixed at compile time that belongs to the class, not to one object. <ulink url="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/static">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/static</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>JSON: a text format used to carry request data; this project uses the jsoncons library to read it. <ulink url="https://jsoncons.github.io/jsoncons/">https://jsoncons.github.io/jsoncons/</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Template (<computeroutput>template &lt;typename T&gt;</computeroutput>): a blueprint that works with whatever data type the caller supplies. <ulink url="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp</ulink></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><simplesect kind="author"><para>Amr Osman <ulink url="mailto:dr3mro@gmail.com">dr3mro@gmail.com</ulink> </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="date"><para>2026-11-16 </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
